torchpme moved `prefactor` from the calculators to the potentials

torch-pme 0.4.0 (2026-01-20) changed where the electrostatic prefactor
is declared.  Its changelog says so outright:

    * ``prefactor`` is now a parameter of the ``Potential`` classes
      instead of the ``Calculator`` classes.

This suite was written against torch-pme 0.3.x and still hands
`prefactor=1.0` to `PMECalculator.__init__`, which raises a TypeError on
anything from 0.4.0 on -- 73 of the torch binding's PME comparison tests
at v0.4.1, every one of them a correctness check against the reference
implementation rather than something cosmetic.

Dropping the argument is exact rather than approximate: `CoulombPotential`
(and `Potential`, which it defers to) already declares
`prefactor: float = 1.0`, so a potential built without it carries the same
prefactor this was asking the calculator for.  There is nothing to move
onto the potential.

The jax call site is included for symmetry.  It does not fail today
because that comparison skips for its own reasons, but it is the same
call against the same API and would fail the moment it stops skipping.
--- a/test/interactions/electrostatics/bindings/jax/test_pme.py
+++ b/test/interactions/electrostatics/bindings/jax/test_pme.py
@@ -204,7 +204,6 @@
         mesh_spacing=mesh_spacing,
         interpolation_nodes=spline_order,
         full_neighbor_list=True,
-        prefactor=1.0,
     ).to(device=device, dtype=dtype)
 
     reciprocal_potential = calculator._compute_kspace(
--- a/test/interactions/electrostatics/bindings/torch/test_pme.py
+++ b/test/interactions/electrostatics/bindings/torch/test_pme.py
@@ -463,7 +463,6 @@
         mesh_spacing=mesh_spacing,
         interpolation_nodes=spline_order,
         full_neighbor_list=True,
-        prefactor=1.0,
     ).to(device=device, dtype=dtype)
 
